"""One-time, resource-scoped owner QA editorial migration into canonical source.

The resulting concept_review_source.json is the regeneration source of truth.
This module documents the approved changes; renderers never overlay this data.
"""
def changes(sources):
    edits={}
    def u(code,**fields):edits.setdefault(code,{}).update(fields)
    def text(code,key):return sources[code]['content'][key]
    def table(code,caption,columns,rows,cells,widths=None):
        u(code,graph=False,table={'caption':caption,'columnHeaders':columns,'rowHeaders':rows,'cells':[[str(v) for v in row] for row in cells],**({'columnWidths':widths} if widths else {})})
    def graph(code,kind):u(code,graph=True,graphSpec={'kind':kind})
    u('GEN-ECON-02',core='Incentives change the expected benefits or costs of a choice, so they can alter behavior at the margin. A congestion fee raises the cost of the next downtown trip. Incentives can be monetary or nonmonetary, and different people may respond differently. A reward can also improve a measured result while harming the underlying goal.',worked='A city adds a $4 congestion fee for a rush-hour trip. Assume other trip costs are already deducted from each driver\'s benefit. A driver with a remaining benefit of $3 avoids the trip because the fee exceeds that benefit. A commuter with a remaining benefit of $10 still travels. The fee changes choices at the margin without making every driver respond alike.')
    u('GEN-ECON-05',core='Microeconomics studies choices by households, firms, workers, and individual markets. Macroeconomics studies economy-wide outcomes such as output, inflation, and unemployment. One event can require both kinds of analysis. The distinction is the unit of analysis, not the size of an organization or dataset.',check='A national retailer studies how its own customers respond to a price increase. Is that question microeconomic or macroeconomic, and why?')
    u('GEN-ECON-08',core='A production possibilities frontier shows the combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its current resources and technology. A bowed-out PPF shows increasing opportunity cost: specialized resources become less well suited as production shifts toward the other good. Points inside indicate unused capacity; points outside are currently infeasible.')
