    u('MICRO-51',core='In a sequential game, one player acts before another. Work backward from the last decision: identify the action that the later player will actually prefer, then use that response to evaluate the first move. A threat is credible only if carrying it out would be worthwhile when the decision arrives. Repeated interaction can make future consequences matter, but no present-value calculation is needed here.',watch='A threatened response is not automatically credible. Compare the later player\'s payoffs at the decision point before using the threat to predict the first move.',worked=text('MICRO-51','worked').replace('The subgame-perfect outcome','The backward-induction outcome'),check='After entry, why will the incumbent accommodate rather than fight, and how does that affect the entrant\'s first decision?')
